{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.domain`\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::Domain` to define its node-identity type. Its\nerror type is introduced in {cite}`requirements.sdk.errors`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject says the application domain implements `Domain` and defines `type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. That gives two testable obligations. A domain must be able to supply an arbitrary `NodeId` that meets those bounds, and the trait must require exactly those bounds. The tests cover both.\n\n- **Exact bounds.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` has an inner generic `check_bounds<G: Domain>`. It calls `identity::<G::NodeId>()`, where `identity<T: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync>`. That compiles only if the trait guarantees every one of those bounds, so loosening any bound would fail the build.\n- **No extra bounds.** The same test's fixture `Identity(u8)` derives only `Clone, PartialEq, Eq`. It has no `Debug`, `Hash`, `Ord` or `Default`. The test implements `Domain` for it and uses it for `node_content`, `outgoing_edges`, `node_path`, and `load_evidence`/`store_evidence`. If the trait added a bound beyond the stated ones, that fixture would stop compiling.\n- **Eq used at runtime.** The same test compares `NodeId` values, for example `successors(...) == [Identity(3), ...]`. It also checks that `node_path` differs between two identities.\n- **Structured `NodeId`.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]` implements `Domain` with an enum `NodeId` (`Requirement(Vec<String>)` and `Source{..}`). It asserts `assert_ne!(requirement, source)`, uses `NodeId` in edges and in dependency records via `first == &requirement`, and stores and loads evidence keyed by `NodeId`.\n- **Real application domains.** These tests use `NodeId` types from the real domain and from test domains:\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[habeas_paths_preserve_opaque_segments_and_separate_all_node_kinds]` uses `HabeasNode` and checks that node paths are distinct.\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` and `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_delegates_unprepared_sources_and_paths_to_the_domain]` also run against `HabeasNode`.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix]` and `#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` use the `Node` test type.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` uses `Application`.\n\nThe `Send + Sync` bounds are enforced at compile time through the generic checks. A negative compile-fail test for non-`Send` identities is not needed, because the bound check already fails if the trait loosens. I found no unmarked or missing scenarios, and nothing is acknowledged by a {fixme}.",
  "references": "8f371c4ec7f0c0885da5414a2e1353bd9a2d1a595b7e6b3d293f799812f87832",
